    "\n",
    "| File | What it is |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| `<studentNumber>.xlsx` | Dashboard workbook — KPI tiles, native (editable) Excel charts, and the supporting data tabs. The Excel mirror of the PDF student report. |\n",
    "| `<studentNumber>_forms.xlsx` | Flat **one row per form** export — the file students ask for so they can compare their own comments, supervisor comments, entrustment, commendations and areas for improvement across every form, with the supervisor who marked each one. |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Choosing sheets.** Every sheet is its own `_sheet*` function, registered in `STUDENT_SHEETS`. Pass `sheets=[...]` to any builder — list order is tab order — or edit the defaults in `DASHBOARD_SHEETS` / `FORM_EXPORT_SHEETS`. Run `listStudentSheets()` for the catalogue. Only the data a chosen sheet needs is queried, so a short list is genuinely cheaper. Unknown sheet names raise immediately rather than silently producing a thinner file.\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "buildStudentWorkbook(engine, \"DDS4\", 1082018, name, path,\n",
    "                     sheets=[\"Dashboard\", \"Summary\", \"Trend\", \"My Forms\"])\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Only curated, human-readable columns are written: no raw JSON (`patient_data` / `student_data` / `assessor_data`), no `*_config`, no schema snapshots, no internal IDs and no e-mail addresses. `_assertNoRawColumns` re-checks every workbook before it is saved.\n",
    "\n",
    "On **My Forms** a coloured band above the headers groups the columns, so students can see that Time Management, Communication, Technical Skills, Person-Centred Care, Professional Behaviour, Risk Management and Knowledge & Clinical Reasoning are the *areas-for-improvement* categories a supervisor files a comment under — a blank column means nothing was raised there, not a zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "Charts are native Excel charts bound to worksheet ranges, so filtering or editing the data updates them. Note the mailer only picks up `.pdf`, so these files are not e-mailed automatically."
